    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 122</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_122/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 23:43:05 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_122/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func main() {&#xA;    var ans float64 = 15 &amp;#43; 25 &amp;#43; 5.2&#xA;    fmt.Println(ans)&#xA;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Answer: 45.2&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 119</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_119/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 23:43:01 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_119/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Consider the following 3 lines of code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the output of the following code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// On a 32-bit machine&#xA;1）var x int32 = 32.0&#xA;2）var y int = x&#xA;3）var z rune = x&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will they compile? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If asked this question in an interview, you need to consider what the interviewer wants to test you on.&#xA;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Reference Answer and Analysis:&#xA;Answer: Cannot compile.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;This is allowed because &lt;code&gt;32.0&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped literal and can be assigned to &lt;code&gt;int32&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; occupies 32 bits on a 32-bit machine and 64 bits on a 64-bit machine. Although &lt;code&gt;int&lt;/code&gt; in the problem occupies the same space as &lt;code&gt;int32&lt;/code&gt;, they are different types and cannot be assigned directly, resulting in a compilation error.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;rune&lt;/code&gt; is an alias for &lt;code&gt;int32&lt;/code&gt;, so it can compile.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 069</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_069/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:44:52 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_069/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Consider the following 3 lines of code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// On a 32-bit machine&#xA;1）var x int32 = 32.0&#xA;2）var y int = x&#xA;3）var z rune = x&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Will they compile? Why?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If asked this question in an interview, you need to consider what the interviewer wants to test you on.&#xA;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;h1 class=&#34;heading-element&#34; id=&#34;numeric-literals&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Numeric Literals&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;  &lt;a href=&#34;#numeric-literals&#34; class=&#34;heading-mark&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;svg class=&#34;octicon octicon-link&#34; viewBox=&#34;0 0 16 16&#34; version=&#34;1.1&#34; width=&#34;16&#34; height=&#34;16&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34;&gt;&lt;path d=&#34;m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z&#34;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Go, literals are untyped. What does untyped mean? Untyped means they can be assigned to variables or constants of similar types. In the example above, &lt;code&gt;32.0&lt;/code&gt; is an untyped floating-point literal, so it can be assigned to any numeric type variable (or constant). All of the following are legal:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 025</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 19:49:56 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_025/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Regarding string concatenation, which of the following syntax is correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;A. str := &amp;#39;abc&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#39;123&amp;#39;&#xA;B. str := &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;123&amp;#34;&#xA;C. str := &amp;#39;123&amp;#39; &amp;#43; &amp;#34;abc&amp;#34;&#xA;D. fmt.Sprintf(&amp;#34;abc%d&amp;#34;, 123)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Answer: BD&#xA;&lt;code&gt;&#39; &#39;&lt;/code&gt; can only be used for single characters, so A and C will not compile.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For string concatenation, &lt;code&gt;buffer.WriteString()&lt;/code&gt; is recommended.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;</description>
    </item>
